--TEST--
The name left of a `::` in type position is read through the alias table first, so an alias standing for an enum names that enum's case — `rule:types/type-alias` and `rule:types/enum-case-type`
--FILE--
<?nvs
enum Mode { Read, Write }

// `rule:types/alias-is-never-a-bare-class` refuses the *fully bare* atom and
// nothing else, so an alias reaches a single name through a wrapping
// construct. That is the one expansion a `::` can be read through: the alias
// stands in for the name before the member is looked up.
type M = (Mode);

class Gate {
    public static function only(M::Read $m): string {
        return "read";
    }

    // The same narrowed type written without the alias. One type, two
    // spellings, because an alias is its expansion everywhere.
    public static function direct(Mode::Read $m): string {
        return "read";
    }
}

echo Gate::only(Mode::Read), "\n";
echo Gate::direct(Mode::Read), "\n";
--EXPECT--
read
read
